  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 108 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nLike a broken record player... the tune of hot and low chances of \nshowers continue through the forecast period. Wednesday and Thursday \nstill like the days with the greatest chances for showers and \nthunderstorms along the coastal plains thanks to an inverted mid-\nlevel trough moving through and with tropical-moisture advecting \nwestward across the Gulf of Mexico. With PWATs in the coastal plains \nforecast to increase to 2.00-2.25\" (near the 90th percentile) and \nthe added ascent from the mid-level trough, any shower that develops \ncan quickly drop 0.5 inch. In the strongest storm, isolated 1-2 inch \naccumulations can't be ruled out, but these higher amounts will \nlikely fall around the Matagorda Bay area. Further inland and into \nthe Brush Country, rain chances will still primarily be seabreeze \ndriven combined with any outflow interactions from storms along the \ncoastal plains, and thus more likely Wednesday and Thursday \nafternoons. \n\nWhile the daily chances for showers has been so desperately needed \nto slow down the spread of drought, unfortunately it comes at the \nexpense of daily hot temperatures, still ranging 5-10F above normal \nvalues for afternoon highs. We'll keep the Moderate to Major \nHeatRisk through Friday, with Extreme risk out in the Brush Country. \nBut come this weekend, we are forecast to see a bigger expansion of \nthis Extreme risk across South Texas. While the increased rain \nchances Wednesday and Thursday may keep the Heat Advisory issuance \nat bay for now, a drier trend may prompt the need for heat headlines \nin the latter-half of the week.",
